<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leicestershire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leicestershire is a county in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, centred on the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it lay in the territory of the Corieltauvi; it later formed part of Mercia and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, and emerged as a shire after the English reconquest of the 10th century. The county was long known for sheep farming, hosiery and footwear, and includes the battlefield of Bosworth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leicestershire.jpg" alt="Leicestershire" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Nugent · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loughborough_Carillon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4228475.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
